            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Storing Data in Your Programs</h2>
                    <p>Every program needs to work with data – names, numbers, dates, and more. In this lesson, you'll learn how to store and manage data using <strong>variables</strong> and understand the different <strong>data types</strong> that C# provides.</p>
                    
                    <p>By the end of this lesson, you'll be able to declare variables, choose the right data type for your needs, and convert between different types.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>What is a Variable?</h2>
                    <p>A <strong>variable</strong> is a named container that stores a value in your computer's memory. Think of it like a labeled box where you can put something, take it out, or replace it with something else.</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Declaring and assigning a variable
string name = "Alice";
int age = 25;
double salary = 55000.50;
bool isEmployed = true;</code></pre>

                    <h3>Variable Declaration Syntax</h3>
                    <p>The basic syntax for declaring a variable is:</p>

                    <div class="syntax-box">
                        <code>type variableName = value;</code>
                    </div>

                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>type</strong> – The kind of data the variable will hold (e.g., <code>int</code>, <code>string</code>)</li>
                        <li><strong>variableName</strong> – The name you give to the variable</li>
                        <li><strong>value</strong> – The initial value (optional at declaration)</li>
                    </ul>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// You can also declare first, assign later
int score;          // Declaration
score = 100;        // Assignment

// Or declare multiple variables of the same type
int x, y, z;
x = 10;
y = 20;
z = 30;

// Or initialize multiple at once
int a = 1, b = 2, c = 3;</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Naming Variables</h2>
                    <p>Choosing good variable names makes your code easier to read and understand. Follow these rules and conventions:</p>

                    <h3>Rules (Must Follow)</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Must start with a letter or underscore (<code>_</code>)</li>
                        <li>Can contain letters, numbers, and underscores</li>
                        <li>Cannot be a C# keyword (like <code>class</code>, <code>int</code>, <code>if</code>)</li>
                        <li>Case-sensitive (<code>Name</code> and <code>name</code> are different)</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h3>Conventions (Best Practices)</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Use <strong>camelCase</strong> for local variables: <code>firstName</code>, <code>totalAmount</code></li>
                        <li>Use meaningful, descriptive names</li>
                        <li>Avoid single letters except for loop counters</li>
                        <li>Don't use abbreviations that aren't widely known</li>
                    </ul>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// ✅ Good variable names
string firstName = "John";
int itemCount = 42;
double accountBalance = 1234.56;
bool isLoggedIn = true;

// ❌ Poor variable names
string s = "John";           // Too short, unclear
int x = 42;                  // What does x represent?
double aB = 1234.56;         // Cryptic abbreviation
bool flag = true;            // Flag for what?</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Built-in Data Types</h2>
                    <p>C# provides many built-in data types. Let's explore the most commonly used ones:</p>

                    <h3>Numeric Types - Integers</h3>
                    <p>For whole numbers (no decimals):</p>

                    <div class="type-table">
                        <div class="type-row header">
                            <span>Type</span>
                            <span>Size</span>
                            <span>Range</span>
                            <span>Example</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="type-row">
                            <code>byte</code>
                            <span>1 byte</span>
                            <span>0 to 255</span>
                            <code>byte b = 255;</code>
                        </div>
                        <div class="type-row">
                            <code>short</code>
                            <span>2 bytes</span>
                            <span>-32,768 to 32,767</span>
                            <code>short s = 1000;</code>
                        </div>
                        <div class="type-row">
                            <code>int</code>
                            <span>4 bytes</span>
                            <span>±2.1 billion</span>
                            <code>int i = 42;</code>
                        </div>
                        <div class="type-row">
                            <code>long</code>
                            <span>8 bytes</span>
                            <span>±9.2 quintillion</span>
                            <code>long l = 9000000000L;</code>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Tip</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>When in doubt, use <code>int</code> for whole numbers. It's the most commonly used and works well for most scenarios.</p>
                    </div>

                    <h3>Numeric Types - Floating Point</h3>
                    <p>For numbers with decimals:</p>

                    <div class="type-table">
                        <div class="type-row header">
                            <span>Type</span>
                            <span>Size</span>
                            <span>Precision</span>
                            <span>Example</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="type-row">
                            <code>float</code>
                            <span>4 bytes</span>
                            <span>~7 digits</span>
                            <code>float f = 3.14f;</code>
                        </div>
                        <div class="type-row">
                            <code>double</code>
                            <span>8 bytes</span>
                            <span>~15 digits</span>
                            <code>double d = 3.14159;</code>
                        </div>
                        <div class="type-row">
                            <code>decimal</code>
                            <span>16 bytes</span>
                            <span>~28 digits</span>
                            <code>decimal m = 19.99m;</code>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Note the suffixes!
float temperature = 98.6f;      // 'f' suffix for float
double pi = 3.14159265359;      // No suffix needed (default)
decimal price = 19.99m;         // 'm' suffix for decimal

// Use decimal for money - it's more precise!
decimal accountBalance = 1234567.89m;</code></pre>

                    <h3>Text Types</h3>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// char - a single character (use single quotes)
char grade = 'A';
char symbol = '@@';
char newline = '\n';

// string - text of any length (use double quotes)
string greeting = "Hello, World!";
string empty = "";
string name = "Alice";

// String with special characters (escape sequences)
string path = "C:\\Users\\Documents";      // Backslash
string quote = "She said \"Hello\"";       // Quotes
string multiline = "Line 1\nLine 2";       // New line

// Verbatim string (@@) - no escaping needed
string verbatimPath = @@"C:\Users\Documents";

// Raw string literals (C# 11+) - for complex strings
string json = """
    {
        "name": "Alice",
        "age": 25
    }
    """;</code></pre>

                    <h3>Boolean Type</h3>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// bool - true or false
bool isActive = true;
bool hasPermission = false;
bool isComplete = true;

// Often used with comparisons
bool isAdult = age >= 18;
bool isEmpty = name.Length == 0;</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>The var Keyword</h2>
                    <p>C# can often figure out the type automatically using <strong>type inference</strong>:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Explicit typing
string message = "Hello";
int count = 42;
double price = 19.99;

// Implicit typing with var
var message = "Hello";      // Compiler knows it's a string
var count = 42;             // Compiler knows it's an int
var price = 19.99;          // Compiler knows it's a double

// var requires initialization
var x;          // ❌ Error! Can't infer type without a value
var y = 10;     // ✅ OK</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>When to use var</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>Use <code>var</code> when the type is obvious from the right side. Use explicit types when clarity is more important or when the type isn't immediately clear.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Constants</h2>
                    <p>A <strong>constant</strong> is a variable whose value cannot change after it's set:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Constants - use 'const' keyword
const double Pi = 3.14159265359;
const int MaxRetries = 3;
const string AppName = "MyApplication";

// Trying to change a constant causes an error
Pi = 3.14;  // ❌ Error! Cannot assign to a constant

// Convention: Use PascalCase for constants</code></pre>

                    <p>Use constants for values that never change, like mathematical constants, configuration values, or magic numbers that have meaning.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Nullable Types</h2>
                    <p>Sometimes a variable might not have a value. C# handles this with <strong>nullable types</strong>:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Regular types cannot be null
int age = null;        // ❌ Error!
string name = null;    // ⚠️ Warning (with nullable enabled)

// Nullable value types - add ? after the type
int? age = null;       // ✅ OK - age might not have a value
int? score = 85;       // ✅ OK - score has a value

// Check if nullable has a value
if (age.HasValue)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Age is {age.Value}");
}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine("Age is unknown");
}

// Null-coalescing operator (??) - provide a default
int displayAge = age ?? 0;  // Use 0 if age is null

// Nullable reference types (string?)
string? middleName = null;  // Explicitly nullable string</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Type Conversion</h2>
                    <p>Sometimes you need to convert between different types:</p>

                    <h3>Implicit Conversion (Automatic)</h3>
                    <p>When there's no risk of data loss, C# converts automatically:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">int myInt = 100;
long myLong = myInt;      // int to long - OK (long is bigger)
double myDouble = myInt;  // int to double - OK

// Small to large: byte → short → int → long → float → double</code></pre>

                    <h3>Explicit Conversion (Casting)</h3>
                    <p>When data might be lost, you must explicitly cast:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">double myDouble = 9.78;
int myInt = (int)myDouble;    // Cast to int - loses decimal part
Console.WriteLine(myInt);      // Output: 9

long bigNumber = 1000;
int smallNumber = (int)bigNumber;  // Might overflow if too large!</code></pre>

                    <h3>Converting To and From Strings</h3>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Converting TO string - use ToString()
int number = 42;
string text = number.ToString();    // "42"

double price = 19.99;
string priceText = price.ToString("C");  // "$19.99" (currency format)

// Converting FROM string - use Parse() or TryParse()
string input = "123";
int value = int.Parse(input);       // Throws if invalid!

// Safer: TryParse returns true/false
string userInput = "456";
if (int.TryParse(userInput, out int result))
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Parsed: {result}");
}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine("Invalid number");
}

// Convert class - another option
int num = Convert.ToInt32("789");
double dbl = Convert.ToDouble("3.14");</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Default Values</h2>
                    <p>Every type has a default value when not explicitly initialized:</p>

                    <div class="type-table">
                        <div class="type-row header">
                            <span>Type</span>
                            <span>Default Value</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="type-row">
                            <span>Numeric types (int, double, etc.)</span>
                            <code>0</code>
                        </div>
                        <div class="type-row">
                            <span>bool</span>
                            <code>false</code>
                        </div>
                        <div class="type-row">
                            <span>char</span>
                            <code>'\0'</code> (null character)
                        </div>
                        <div class="type-row">
                            <span>Reference types (string, object)</span>
                            <code>null</code>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Using the default keyword
int number = default;      // 0
bool flag = default;       // false
string text = default;     // null

// Or with the type specified
var x = default(int);      // 0</code></pre>
                </section>

